# 05. SCROLL EXPERIENCE — EXTREMELY IMPORTANT

The scrolling experience is one of the highest priorities.

Do not build five ordinary sections stacked vertically.

Create a continuous cinematic journey.

Use:
- GSAP ScrollTrigger.
- Scroll-scrubbed timelines.
- Pinned sections.
- Smooth camera movements.
- Layered parallax.
- Split-text reveals.
- Clip-path transitions.
- Staggered object appearances.
- Scroll-controlled object rotation.
- Horizontal movement inside selected chapters.
- Scale and depth transitions.

SCROLL BEHAVIOUR:

A. When the visitor enters a chapter, the environment should gradually transform.

B. Keep important 3D objects visible while explanatory text moves around them.

C. Pin selected scenes while the visitor scrolls through their story.

D. Let scroll progress directly control camera movement and object transformations.

E. Use horizontal scrolling only in selected timeline or artifact sequences, not everywhere.

F. Reveal information in a clear, readable order.

G. Scrolling backwards should reverse scroll-linked animations naturally.

H. Never hijack normal browser scrolling.

I. Do not force visitors to wait through long animations.

J. Do not make all elements animate simultaneously.

Create a consistent cinematic rhythm:
- Establish the environment.
- Introduce the historical event.
- Reveal the artifacts.
- Allow interaction.
- Present the decision.
- Show its consequence.
- Transition into the next era.

The visitor must always understand where they are in the story.

